, and by week 42 there was a great increase in the proportion of methanogens which are microorganisms that produce methane, the major component of biogas. No pronounced shift in the composition of the microbial community were observed due to either air or sound treatment. Rather, microbial diversity was enhanced relative to the control. It is likely that both treatments aid in waste breakdown and improve nutrient availability to promote growth and increase biogas production.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="FAESRU.JL.8" accession="SRS18813872"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS18813872</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN37298558</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|getahun.agga@usda.gov" label="Sample name">FAESRU.JL.8</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>1263854</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>anaerobic digester metagenome</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>1013451</ID><LABEL>PRJNA1013451</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>isolation_source</TAG><VALUE>anaerobic digestion of poultry litter</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>collection_date</TAG><VALUE>2017-07-17</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>geo_loc_name</TAG><VALUE>USA: Kentucky, Bowling Green</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>lat_lon</TAG><VALUE>36.5854 N 86.2640 W</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>source_material_id</TAG><VALUE>Air &amp; Sound, Week 23</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>Metagenome or environmental</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES></SAMPLE><Pool><Member member_name="" accession="SRS18813872" sample_name="FAESRU.JL.8" spots="21634604" bases="6533650408" tax_id="1263854" organism="anaerobic digester metagenome"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS18813872</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN37298558</EXTERNAL_ID></IDENTIFIERS